## Authentication

{{#hasSchemeCredentials}}
Configure one optional credential per security scheme on the client, by name: {{#schemeCredentials}}`{{param}}`{{^-last}}, {{/-last}}{{/schemeCredentials}} (`new {{clientClassName}}({{#schemeCredentials}}{{#-first}}{{param}}: $credential{{/-first}}{{/schemeCredentials}})`). Each operation uses the scheme its security requirement names; there is no per-call authenticator.

{{/hasSchemeCredentials}}
All authentication is handled via `Authenticator` implementations passed to the client constructor.

### Bearer Token

```php statements
use {{invokerPackage}}\{{clientClassName}};
use {{invokerPackage}}\Auth\BearerAuthenticator;

$authenticator = new BearerAuthenticator('https://api.example.com', 'your-token');
$client = new {{clientClassName}}($authenticator);
```

A generated authenticator of a basic, bearer, API key or OAuth 2 scheme reads each credential from an environment variable when you pass `null` (read once when the authenticator is built, an empty value counts as unset, a missing credential throws `InvalidArgumentException`; an argument always wins):

{{#credentialSchemeEnvVars}}
- `{{className}}` reads `{{envName}}` for `{{field}}`.
{{/credentialSchemeEnvVars}}
{{^credentialSchemeEnvVars}}
- This API declares no basic, bearer or API key scheme, so no variable is read.
{{/credentialSchemeEnvVars}}

The prefix `{{envVarPrefix}}` comes from the `envVarPrefix` generator option. Pass a `\Closure` as the third argument of `BearerAuthenticator` to supply a rotating token on every request. The base classes read no variable; the OAuth 2 and OpenID Connect authenticators take their credentials as arguments.

