{{#hasSseStreamOperation}}

```php file
use {{invokerPackage}}\{{clientClassName}};
{{#sseStreamOperation}}
{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}
{{#isOptions}}
use {{invokerPackage}}\Api\Options\{{{dataType}}};
{{/isOptions}}
{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}

function followEvents({{clientClassName}} $client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{#isPath}}{{{dataType}}} ${{paramName}}{{/isPath}}{{#isBody}}{{{dataType}}}{{#nullable}}|null{{/nullable}} ${{paramName}}{{/isBody}}{{#isOptions}}{{#nullable}}?{{/nullable}}{{{dataType}}} $options{{/isOptions}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}): void
{
    $feed = $client->{{#sseStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/sseStreamGroup}}->{{vendorExtensions.op.streamMethodName}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}{{^-first}}, {{/-first}}${{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}});
    try {
        foreach ($feed as $event) {
            echo $event->raw . "\n";
        }
    } finally {
        $feed->close();
    }
}
{{/sseStreamOperation}}
```
{{/hasSseStreamOperation}}

An event stream reconnects when the connection ends, fails or stays silent for the idle timeout: it asks again with fresh headers and `Last-Event-ID`, waits the server's `retry` value or `reconnectDelay` (1 second, doubling to 30 seconds with jitter), and raises the last failure after `maxReconnects` failures in a row without an event (5; 0 for a request with a body; `-1` is unlimited). It never reconnects after a 204, a status other than 408, 429 and 5xx, a response that is not `text/event-stream`, a cancellation or `client.close()`. An event named `error` raises `ApiException` with the response status and the data as its body, and a comment line such as a heartbeat counts as activity for the idle timeout. The generator option `sseTerminator` names a data value (such as `[DONE]`) that ends the stream and is not delivered. `StreamOptions` also takes `idleTimeout` and `lastEventId`.

### Custom HTTP client

```php file
use {{invokerPackage}}\{{clientClassName}};
use {{invokerPackage}}\Psr18ApiClient;
use Psr\Http\Client\ClientInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\RequestFactoryInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\StreamFactoryInterface;

/* $httpClient is any PSR-18 client with redirects disabled; $psr17 is any PSR-17 factory. */
function clientWithHttpClient(
    ClientInterface $httpClient,
    RequestFactoryInterface&StreamFactoryInterface $psr17,
    string $token
): {{clientClassName}} {
    $apiClient = new Psr18ApiClient($httpClient, $psr17, $psr17);

    return {{clientClassName}}::withToken('https://api.example.com', $token, null, $apiClient);
}
```

The SDK sends every request through your client and never closes it. Combining a supplied client with `proxy`, `caCertPath` or `verifySsl(false)` throws `InvalidArgumentException`.

### Per-call options

Every operation takes an options object as its last argument, including one with no parameters and no security scheme, and it carries `timeout` (ms), `headers` and `maxRetries` for that call only, replacing the transport settings.

{{#hasExampleOperation}}
{{#exampleOperation}}
```php file
use {{invokerPackage}}\{{clientClassName}};
use {{invokerPackage}}\Api\Options\{{vendorExtensions.op.optionsClassName}};

function {{operationId}}WithinTwoSeconds({{clientClassName}} $client): void
{
    $client->{{#exampleOperationGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/exampleOperationGroup}}->{{operationId}}(new {{vendorExtensions.op.optionsClassName}}(timeout: 2000, headers: ['X-Tenant' => 'acme'], maxRetries: 0));
}
```
{{/exampleOperation}}
{{/hasExampleOperation}}

### Cancellation

The SDK has no per-call cancellation. Bound a call with `TransportOptions::builder()->timeout(5000)`; a call that exceeds it throws `NetworkTimeoutException`.

### Raw responses, timeouts and the request id

Each operation has a `...WithHttpInfo` variant returning an `ApiResult` (`statusCode`, `data`, `rawBody`, `headers`). `timeout` is one end-to-end budget in milliseconds. `TransportOptions::builder()->injectRequestId(true)` adds an `X-Request-ID` header to each request unless you set one.

### Hooks and telemetry

One optional hook runs around every call: `{{clientClassName}}::withToken($host, $token, callHook: $hook)` (or `Configuration::builder()->callHook($hook)`). `CallHook::start($info, $headers)` runs once per call after the headers are final and may edit them; it returns a `CallObserver` (or `null`) whose `finish($outcome)` runs exactly once with the status (`0` when no response arrived), the lower-cased headers (`null` when none arrived) and the error. An error from `start` reaches the caller; an error from `finish` is swallowed. `CallHookChain::chain($a, $b)` starts in order and finishes in reverse. `OpenTelemetryCallHook::create()` records one span, `http.client.request.count` and `http.client.request.duration` per call; it needs `open-telemetry/api` (`composer require open-telemetry/api`).

## API Methods

Each API group is exposed as a typed property on the client (e.g., `$client->{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`). API classes have methods that correspond to OpenAPI operations, accepting typed request parameters and returning typed response models.

## Models

Models are generated as PHP classes under the `{{invokerPackage}}\Models` namespace. They are immutable: properties are `readonly` and `withX()` returns a changed copy. A `oneOf` or `anyOf` wrapper has `fromX()`, `isX()` and `asX()` per variant, and an operation with several 2xx responses returns such a wrapper decoded by the status code that arrived.

{{#models}}{{#model}}{{#-first}}{{^isEnum}}{{#vars}}{{#-first}}```php file
use {{invokerPackage}}\Models\{{classname}};

function {{vendorExtensions.withMethod}}From({{classname}} $model, {{classname}} $other): {{classname}}
{
    return $model->{{vendorExtensions.withMethod}}($other->{{name}});
}
```{{/-first}}{{/vars}}{{/isEnum}}{{/-first}}{{/model}}{{/models}}

## Binary / File Uploads

A multipart file field is a `\SplFileObject` (or a PSR-7 stream). A binary request body is a `\SplFileObject`, a PSR-7 `StreamInterface` or a `string` of raw bytes, so in-memory content needs no temporary file. A binary response body is returned as a `ByteStream`, a PSR-7 `StreamInterface` (see Streaming).

## Comment Style

Never place a comment on the same line as code. Use block comments (`/* ... */`); PHPDoc (`/** ... */`) is fine.

```good
/* This explains the logic */
$x = 1;
```

```bad
// This explains the logic
$x = 1;
```
